      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the length of the longest side of a similar triangle whose perimeter is 110 cm. We start by determining the length of the longest side of the original triangle, which has side lengths 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm.\n\nFirst, we calculate the perimeter of the original triangle:\n\\[\n6 + 7 + 9 = 22 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles. The perimeter of the similar triangle is 110 cm, so the ratio of the perimeters is:\n\\[\n\\frac{110}{22} = 5\n\\]\n\nSince the triangles are similar, the ratio of the corresponding sides is also 5. Therefore, the longest side of the original triangle, which is 9 cm, will have a corresponding side in the similar triangle that is 5 times longer. We calculate this length as follows:\n\\[\n9 \\times 5 = 45 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}\n\\]",
