      "text": "To find the length of the longest side of the similar triangle, we first need to determine the perimeter of the original triangle. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its three sides. For the original triangle with sides 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm, the perimeter is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n6 + 7 + 9 = 22 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThe similar triangle has a perimeter of 110 cm. Since the triangles are similar, the ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles is the same as the ratio of the corresponding sides. Let's denote the ratio of the sides of the similar triangle to the original triangle as \\( k \\). The perimeter of the similar triangle is 110 cm, and the perimeter of the original triangle is 22 cm. Therefore, the ratio \\( k \\) is:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{110}{22} = 5\n\\]\n\nThis means that each side of the similar triangle is 5 times the length of the corresponding side of the original triangle. The longest side of the original triangle has a length of 9 cm. Therefore, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\n\\[\n9 \\times 5 = 45 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is \\(\\boxed{45}\\).",
